<item>
            <title>[LU-20914] LOD OST and MDT statfs updates share one age, so either can delay the other</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20914</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;lod_qos_statfs_update() refreshes the statfs data of all the targets in a table (lod_ost_descs or lod_mdt_descs) at most every 2 * qos_maxage of that table, but it checks and sets the age in lod2obd(lod)-&amp;gt;obd_osfs_age, which both tables share.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A striped mkdir with the default stripe offset, or a directory split, runs lod_qos_statfs_update(lod_mdt_descs), which refreshes obd_osfs_age without refreshing any OST statfs.  The next file create then skips lod_qos_statfs_update(lod_ost_descs) because the age looks recent.  With frequent striped mkdirs, and in particular with mdt_qos_maxage lower than qos_maxage, the OST statfs data used for QoS weights and the OST active checks can go unrefreshed indefinitely.  File creates likewise delay the MDT statfs refresh for striped directories.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This matters more once inactive OSTs are left out of the round-robin array (&lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-8933&quot; title=&quot;sanity test_51d: object distribution between OSTs not even&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-8933&quot;&gt;LU-8933&lt;/a&gt;), since the periodic statfs update is then what notices that an OST is usable again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Fix: keep a separate statfs age per target table, in struct lu_qos.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
